INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(88,28,'bot','text','*Greets warmly* Hello Agnish, it''s a pleasure to meet you! I''m the Meridian Enterprise AI Copilot, powered by AWS Bedrock in the ap-south-1 region. I''d be happy to assist you with a variety of tasks - from Zoom troubleshooting and Windows camera/microphone privacy permissions, to invoicing and GST tax code guidelines, as well as Jira L2 Support escalation. Please let me know how I can be of help, and I''ll do my best to provide efficient and professional support. I look forward to working with you!','2026-09-23 10:48:09.521350');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(89,28,'user','text','my zoom is not working?','2026-09-23 10:49:26.305365');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(90,28,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "I understand you are having issues with your Zoom application. Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide in the Confluence knowledge base, here are the steps I recommend to resolve the problem:\n\n1. Clear the Zoom Application Cache:\n   a. Close Zoom completely and ensure no Zoom processes are running in the background.\n   b. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders).\n   c. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''.\n   d. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''.\n   e. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in.\n\n2. Perform a Clean Reinstall of Zoom:\n   a. Close Zoom completely.\n   b. Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps, find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', and click Uninstall.\n   c. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\Zoom.\n   d. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package.\n   e. Relaunch Zoom and log in.\n\n3. Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes:\n   a. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager.\n   b. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes and click ''End Task'' for each.\n   c. Reopen Zoom from the Start Menu.\n\n4. Check Network Stability and Firewall:\n   a. Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser and running a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls).\n   b. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly.\n   c. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or using an ethernet cable.\n\n5. Disable Hardware Acceleration in Zoom Video Settings:\n   a. Open Zoom Settings (gear icon in the top right), click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''.\n   b. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''.\n   c. Restart Zoom.\n\n6. Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions:\n   a", "matches": [{"score": 0.6136, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide - Video Conferencing. Symptoms: Zoom application freezes or becomes unresponsive upon opening, Zoom crashes unexpectedly during or before meetings, Zoom crashes immediately when joining a meeting or starting video, Zoom window turns white or black and closes without an error message, Audio not working in Zoom (can''t hear or be heard), Video/camera not working in Zoom or showing blank display, Unable to join Zoom meetings with connection timeout, Zoom is running slow, dropping frames, or lagging severely, Screen sharing fails or causes the application to terminate, Zoom login issues or authentication session tokens corrupted, Zoom updates failing or corrupt installation state, Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart. Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache. Zoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom. Over time or after improper application termination, these files become corrupted, causing instant crashes when Zoom tries to initialize the UI or join meetings. Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch. 1. Close Zoom completely (check the system tray to ensure no background Zoom processes are running).\n2. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter.\n3. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders).\n4. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''.\n5. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''.\n6. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in. Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom. If clearing the cache does not resolve the issue, a full clean reinstall removes all Zoom components (including potentially corrupted binaries, DLLs, and registry entries) and installs a fresh copy of the latest version from the Microsoft Store or official package repository. This resolves deep binary corruption, version conflicts, and persistent crashes. 1. Close Zoom completely.\n2. Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps.\n3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall.\n4. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\Zoom.\n5. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package.\n6. Relaunch Zoom and log in. Step 3: Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes. A hanging ZoomOpener or CptHost process in Task Manager can lock Zoom data files, preventing a new instance from opening properly. 1. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager.\n2. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes.\n3. Select each and click ''End Task''.\n4. Reopen Zoom from the Start Menu. Step 4: Check Network Stability & Firewall. Zoom requires an active and stable network connection with open UDP ports. Packet loss or aggressive proxy filters can cause crashes during the meeting handshake. 1. Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser.\n2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls).\n3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly.\n4. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or ethernet cable. Step 5: Disable Hardware Acceleration in Zoom Video Settings. Incompatible or outdated GPU display drivers frequently cause Zoom to crash or display a black/white screen when opening video. 1. Open Zoom Settings (gear icon in the top right).\n2. Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''.\n3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''.\n4. Restart Zoom. Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions. Windows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media. 1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom.\n3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom.\n4. Relaunch Zoom. Escalation: If preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies."}, {"score": 0.3607, "title": "Windows 11 Microphone & Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341185", "text": "Windows 11 Microphone & Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting - Audio & Video / Privacy Settings. Symptoms: Microphone not working in video conferencing apps (Teams, Zoom, Meet), Camera shows black/blocked in video calls, Windows says microphone access is blocked or denied, App says ''We can''t access your microphone'', Camera privacy indicator shows access is off, Audio routing not working \u2014 can hear but can''t be heard, Microphone works in some apps but not others, Windows 11 privacy settings blocking mic or camera, Headset microphone not detected in meeting apps, Camera permission denied by operating system. Step 1: Check Windows Privacy Settings for Microphone. Windows 11 has privacy controls that can block apps from accessing the microphone. These settings are OS-level and must be changed manually through the Windows Settings UI \u2014 they cannot be safely overridden by background scripts as it would violate operating system security policies. 1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Microphone.\n3. Ensure ''Microphone access'' is turned ON.\n4. Ensure ''Let apps access your microphone'' is turned ON.\n5. Scroll down and ensure the specific app (Teams, Zoom, etc.) has microphone access enabled.\n6. If using a desktop app, ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is turned ON at the bottom. Step 2: Check Windows Privacy Settings for Camera. Similar to microphone, Windows 11 has privacy controls for camera access that must be manually configured. 1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Camera.\n3. Ensure ''Camera access'' is turned ON.\n4. Ensure ''Let apps access your camera'' is turned ON.\n5. Scroll down and ensure the specific app has camera access enabled.\n6. If using a desktop app, ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is turned ON. Step 3: Check Sound Settings and Default Device. The wrong audio input/output device may be selected as the default, or the microphone volume may be muted. 1. Right-click the speaker icon in the taskbar and select ''Sound settings''.\n2. Under ''Input'', check that the correct microphone is selected.\n3. Click on the microphone and ensure the volume is not at 0.\n4. Test the microphone by speaking \u2014 the input level indicator should move.\n5. Under ''Output'', verify the correct speakers/headset is selected. Step 4: Run Windows Audio Troubleshooter. Windows has a built-in troubleshooter that can detect and fix common audio problems. 1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Go to System > Troubleshoot > Other troubleshooters.\n3. Find ''Recording Audio'' and click ''Run''.\n4. Follow the on-screen instructions.\n5. Restart the affected app after the troubleshooter completes. Step 5: Escalate to L2 Support \u2014 Remote Desktop Session. If the user is unable to navigate Windows privacy settings or the issue persists after manual checks, escalate to a Level 2 support technician who can connect via a secure, time-limited remote desktop session to visually resolve the issue. This step is handled by the AI \u2014 it will create a secure 30-minute remote desktop sharing link for a support technician to connect and resolve the issue visually. Escalation: Issues involving Windows OS-level privacy settings (microphone/camera permissions) and audio routing configurations require visual human intervention. The AI should identify this boundary and open an L2 support ticket with an ephemeral remote desktop sharing link for a technician to resolve the issue."}, {"score": 0.1579, "title": "How to raise an invoice for a product", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793", "text": "Overview This article explains the standard steps to raise an invoice for any product already configured in the system. Steps Go to Invoices > Raise Invoice. Select the product, enter quantity, and choose the correct tax code. Add any remarks if applicable, then click Submit. Confirm the invoice appears under ''My Invoices'' with status ''Submitted''."}]}','2026-09-23 10:49:26.305365');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(91,28,'bot','resolved','{"status": "Resolved", "note": "Employee confirmed query was successfully resolved."}','2026-09-23 10:49:35.672843');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(92,28,'user','text','how to raise an  invoice?','2026-09-23 10:49:49.316446');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(93,28,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Here are the steps to raise an invoice for a product:\n\n1. Go to the Invoices section and select \"Raise Invoice\".\n2. On the Raise Invoice screen, select the product you need to invoice.\n3. Enter the quantity for the product.\n4. Choose the correct tax code for the product. If the product is subject to GST at 28%, make sure to select the appropriate GST 28% tax code.\n5. Add any relevant remarks or notes in the Remarks field, if applicable.\n6. Review the invoice details and then click \"Submit\" to finalize the invoice.\n\nOnce submitted, the invoice will have a status of \"Submitted\" and will appear under the \"My Invoices\" section. \n\nKey points to remember:\n\n- Make sure to select the correct client before uploading any tax documents, as these are tied to the specific client.\n- Missing or incorrect tax documentation may delay the invoice approval process.\n- Submitted invoices cannot be edited directly - they must go through the approval workflow first.\n- If an invoice is rejected, it will return to \"Draft\" status with reviewer comments for you to address.\n\nLet me know if you have any other questions!", "matches": [{"score": 0.7277, "title": "How to raise an invoice for a product", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793", "text": "Overview This article explains the standard steps to raise an invoice for any product already configured in the system. Steps Go to Invoices > Raise Invoice. Select the product, enter quantity, and choose the correct tax code. Add any remarks if applicable, then click Submit. Confirm the invoice appears under ''My Invoices'' with status ''Submitted''."}, {"score": 0.5151, "title": "Uploading client tax documents", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354", "text": "Overview Each client may have specific tax documentation on file, such as a tax exemption certificate or GST registration certificate for their jurisdiction. Steps Select the client before uploading their document - documents are tied to the specific client, not the invoice. Upload the relevant certificate in the Tax Document field on the Raise Invoice screen. If you switch to a different client, you must upload that client''s own document - a previous client''s file does not carry over. Note Missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay invoice approval."}, {"score": 0.469, "title": "Invoice approval and submission workflow", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441820", "text": "Overview Once submitted, invoices go through the following stages: Draft, Submitted, Under Review, Approved. Steps Draft invoices can be edited freely. Once submitted, an invoice is locked and routed for review. If rejected, the invoice returns to Draft with reviewer comments."}]}','2026-09-23 10:49:49.316446');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(94,29,'user','text','What are the steps to fix microphone and camera privacy blocked?','2026-09-23 11:04:50.539775');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(95,29,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "To fix microphone and camera privacy issues in Windows 11, please follow these steps:\n\n1. Check Windows Privacy Settings for Microphone:\n   a. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n   b. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Microphone.\n   c. Ensure ''Microphone access'' is turned ON.\n   d. Ensure ''Let apps access your microphone'' is turned ON.\n   e. Scroll down and ensure the specific app (Teams, Zoom, etc.) has microphone access enabled.\n   f. If using a desktop app, ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is turned ON.\n\n2. Check Windows Privacy Settings for Camera:\n   a. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n   b. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Camera.\n   c. Ensure ''Camera access'' is turned ON.\n   d. Ensure ''Let apps access your camera'' is turned ON.\n   e. Scroll down and ensure the specific app has camera access enabled.\n   f. If using a desktop app, ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is turned ON.\n\n3. Check Sound Settings and Default Device:\n   a. Right-click the speaker icon in the taskbar and select ''Sound settings''.\n   b. Under ''Input'', check that the correct microphone is selected.\n   c. Click on the microphone and ensure the volume is not at 0.\n   d. Test the microphone by speaking \u2014 the input level indicator should move.\n   e. Under ''Output'', verify the correct speakers/headset is selected.\n\n4. Run Windows Audio Troubleshooter:\n   a. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n   b. Go to System > Troubleshoot > Other troubleshooters.\n   c. Find the ''Playing audio'' troubleshooter and click ''Run''.\n   d. Follow the on-screen instructions to diagnose and fix any audio-related issues.\n\nNote: The privacy settings mentioned in steps 1 and 2 are OS-level security controls and must be changed manually through the Windows Settings UI. Automated scripts should never override these settings, as it would violate operating system security policies.\n\nIf the issue persists after following these steps, please escalate the ticket to L2 Support for further assistance.", "matches": [{"score": 0.5995, "title": "Windows 11 Microphone  Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting", "url": "https://botdata1.s3.amazonaws.com/Windows_11_Microphone__Camera_Privacy_Permissions_Troubleshooting.md", "text": "* Camera permission denied by operating system\n\n## Troubleshooting Steps\n\n### Step 1: Check Windows Privacy Settings for Microphone\n\nWindows 11 has privacy controls that can block apps from accessing the microphone. These settings are OS-level and must be changed manually through the Windows Settings UI \u2014 they cannot be safely overridden by background scripts as it would violate operating system security policies.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Microphone.\n3. Ensure ''Microphone access'' is turned ON.\n4. Ensure ''Let apps access your microphone'' is turned ON.\n5. Scroll down and ensure the specific app (Teams, Zoom, etc.) has microphone access enabled.\n6. If using a desktop app, ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is turned ON at the bottom.\n\n> **Note:** These privacy settings are protected by Windows security policies and MUST be changed through the UI by the user or a support technician. Automated scripts should never silently override OS privacy controls.\n\n### Step 2: Check Windows Privacy Settings for Camera\n\nSimilar to microphone, Windows 11 has privacy controls for camera access that must be manually configured.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Camera.\n3. Ensure ''Camera access'' is turned ON.\n4."}, {"score": 0.5765, "title": "Windows 11 Microphone  Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting", "url": "https://botdata1.s3.amazonaws.com/Windows_11_Microphone__Camera_Privacy_Permissions_Troubleshooting.md", "text": "# Windows 11 Microphone & Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting\n\n**Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341185\n**Page ID:** 5341185\n**Document ID:** KB-AV-001 | **Category:** Audio & Video / Privacy Settings | **Application:** Windows 11 System Settings\n**Last Updated:** 2026-09-16 | **Author:** IT Support Team\n\n**Tags:** microphone, camera, privacy, permissions, windows 11, audio, not working, blocked, teams, zoom, meet, recording, access denied, audio routing\n\n## Reported Symptoms\n\n* Microphone not working in video conferencing apps (Teams, Zoom, Meet)\n* Camera shows black/blocked in video calls\n* Windows says microphone access is blocked or denied\n* App says ''We can''t access your microphone''\n* Camera privacy indicator shows access is off\n* Audio routing not working \u2014 can hear but can''t be heard\n* Microphone works in some apps but not others\n* Windows 11 privacy settings blocking mic or camera\n* Headset microphone not detected in meeting apps\n* Camera permission denied by operating system\n\n## Troubleshooting Steps\n\n### Step 1: Check Windows Privacy Settings for Microphone"}, {"score": 0.5398, "title": "Windows 11 Microphone  Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting", "url": "https://botdata1.s3.amazonaws.com/Windows_11_Microphone__Camera_Privacy_Permissions_Troubleshooting.md", "text": "Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Navigate to Privacy & Security > Camera.\n3. Ensure ''Camera access'' is turned ON.\n4. Ensure ''Let apps access your camera'' is turned ON.\n5. Scroll down and ensure the specific app has camera access enabled.\n6. If using a desktop app, ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is turned ON.\n\n> **Note:** Camera privacy settings are OS-level security controls and must not be overridden by scripts.\n\n### Step 3: Check Sound Settings and Default Device\n\nThe wrong audio input/output device may be selected as the default, or the microphone volume may be muted.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Right-click the speaker icon in the taskbar and select ''Sound settings''.\n2. Under ''Input'', check that the correct microphone is selected.\n3. Click on the microphone and ensure the volume is not at 0.\n4. Test the microphone by speaking \u2014 the input level indicator should move.\n5. Under ''Output'', verify the correct speakers/headset is selected.\n\n### Step 4: Run Windows Audio Troubleshooter\n\nWindows has a built-in troubleshooter that can detect and fix common audio problems.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Go to System > Troubleshoot > Other troubleshooters.\n3."}, {"score": 0.5042, "title": "Windows 11 Microphone  Camera Privacy Permissions Troubleshooting", "url": "https://botdata1.s3.amazonaws.com/Windows_11_Microphone__Camera_Privacy_Permissions_Troubleshooting.md", "text": "**Instructions:**\n1. Open Windows Settings (Win + I).\n2. Go to System > Troubleshoot > Other troubleshooters.\n3. Find ''Recording Audio'' and click ''Run''.\n4. Follow the on-screen instructions.\n5. Restart the affected app after the troubleshooter completes.\n\n### Step 5: Escalate to L2 Support \u2014 Remote Desktop Session\n\nIf the user is unable to navigate Windows privacy settings or the issue persists after manual checks, escalate to a Level 2 support technician who can connect via a secure, time-limited remote desktop session to visually resolve the issue.\n\n**Instructions:**\nThis step is handled by the AI \u2014 it will create a secure 30-minute remote desktop sharing link for a support technician to connect and resolve the issue visually.\n\n> **Note:** A human technician will connect via a 30-minute self-expiring web remote desktop link. No client software is needed. The link automatically revokes after the time window.\n\n## Escalation Guidelines\n\nIssues involving Windows OS-level privacy settings (microphone/camera permissions) and audio routing configurations require visual human intervention. The AI should identify this boundary and open an L2 support ticket with an ephemeral remote desktop sharing link for a technician to resolve the issue."}, {"score": 0.3955, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''.\n3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''.\n4. Restart Zoom.\n\n### Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions\n\nWindows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom.\n3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom.\n4. Relaunch Zoom.\n\n## Escalation Guidelines\n\nIf preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies."}]}','2026-09-23 11:04:50.539775');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(96,30,'user','text','My Zoom application keeps freezing and crashing when I open it, how do I fix it?','2026-09-23 11:36:14.111102');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(97,30,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide provided, I can provide the following step-by-step guidance to resolve the issue with your Zoom application crashing:\n\n1. Clear the Zoom Application Cache:\n   a. Close the Zoom application completely, including any background processes.\n   b. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter.\n   c. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders).\n   d. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''.\n   e. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''.\n   f. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in.\n\n   Note: This will sign you out of Zoom, so you will need to log in again after clearing the cache.\n\n2. If the issue persists after clearing the cache, proceed with a clean reinstall of the Zoom application:\n   a. Uninstall the existing Zoom application.\n   b. Download the latest version of the Zoom client from the official website.\n   c. Install the new Zoom client, making sure to allow all necessary permissions and configurations.\n   d. Launch the Zoom application and sign in.\n\nIf the Zoom application continues to crash or freeze even after the cache clearing and clean reinstall, you may need to escalate the issue to L2 Support for further investigation. The troubleshooting guide also mentions that issues like this could be caused by GPU hardware acceleration conflicts or background process locks, so L2 Support may need to perform additional diagnostics.\n\nPlease let me know if you have any other questions or if the issue persists after following these steps.", "matches": [{"score": 0.5454, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "* Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart\n\n## Troubleshooting Steps\n\n### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache\n\nZoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom. Over time or after improper application termination, these files become corrupted, causing instant crashes when Zoom tries to initialize the UI or join meetings. Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Close Zoom completely (check the system tray to ensure no background Zoom processes are running).\n2. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter.\n3. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders).\n4. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''.\n5. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''.\n6. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in.\n\n> **Note:** This will sign you out of Zoom. You will need to log in again after clearing the cache.\n\n### Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom"}, {"score": 0.5246, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "* When exactly does the issue occur? (e.g., Immediately upon launching Zoom, during sign-in, when clicking ''Join Meeting'', or when starting video/screen sharing?)\n* Do you see any error messages or error codes (such as Error 10003, Error 3000, or a Windows application crash notification), or does Zoom close abruptly without warning?\n* Did this problem start happening recently (for example, following a recent Zoom client update, Windows update, or system reboot)?\n\n## Reported Symptoms\n\n* Zoom application freezes or becomes unresponsive upon opening\n* Zoom crashes unexpectedly during or before meetings\n* Zoom crashes immediately when joining a meeting or starting video\n* Zoom window turns white or black and closes without an error message\n* Audio not working in Zoom (can''t hear or be heard)\n* Video/camera not working in Zoom or showing blank display\n* Unable to join Zoom meetings with connection timeout\n* Zoom is running slow, dropping frames, or lagging severely\n* Screen sharing fails or causes the application to terminate\n* Zoom login issues or authentication session tokens corrupted\n* Zoom updates failing or corrupt installation state\n* Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart\n\n## Troubleshooting Steps\n\n### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache\n\nZoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom."}, {"score": 0.5127, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "# Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\n\n**Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\n**Page ID:** 5341202\n**Document ID:** KB-ZOOM-001 | **Category:** Video Conferencing | **Application:** Zoom\n**Last Updated:** 2026-09-21 | **Author:** IT Support Team\n\n**Tags:** zoom, video conferencing, freezing, crashing, audio, video, camera, microphone, cache, not working, can''t join, meeting, lag, slow, black screen, screen share, reinstall, uninstall, corrupt, error 10003, crash on launch\n\n## Preliminary Assessment\n\nBefore proceeding directly to cache clearing or reinstallation, it is essential to ask the user diagnostic questions to identify whether the crash is triggered by corrupted temporary cache files, GPU hardware acceleration conflicts, or background process locks. In the vast majority of cases where Zoom crashes abruptly during launch or joining calls without a system hardware fault, corrupted local AppData cache files (CEF webcache, meeting session data, and dump logs) are the primary culprit.\n\n## Diagnostic Questions\n\n* When exactly does the issue occur? (e.g., Immediately upon launching Zoom, during sign-in, when clicking ''Join Meeting'', or when starting video/screen sharing?)"}, {"score": 0.4624, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "> **Note:** This will sign you out of Zoom. You will need to log in again after clearing the cache.\n\n### Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom\n\nIf clearing the cache does not resolve the issue, a full clean reinstall removes all Zoom components (including potentially corrupted binaries, DLLs, and registry entries) and installs a fresh copy of the latest version from the Microsoft Store or official package repository. This resolves deep binary corruption, version conflicts, and persistent crashes.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Close Zoom completely.\n2. Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps.\n3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall.\n4. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\Zoom.\n5. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package.\n6. Relaunch Zoom and log in.\n\n> **Note:** This will completely remove Zoom and all its settings/data. The application will be reinstalled fresh. Custom settings will need to be reconfigured.\n\n### Step 3: Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes\n\nA hanging ZoomOpener or CptHost process in Task Manager can lock Zoom data files, preventing a new instance from opening properly.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager.\n2. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes.\n3."}, {"score": 0.4605, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''.\n3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''.\n4. Restart Zoom.\n\n### Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions\n\nWindows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media.\n\n**Instructions:**\n1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom.\n3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom.\n4. Relaunch Zoom.\n\n## Escalation Guidelines\n\nIf preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies."}]}','2026-09-23 11:36:14.111102');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(98,30,'bot','jira_ticket','{"is_live_jira": true, "ticket_key": "KAN-5", "ticket_url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/browse/KAN-5", "summary": "[Support Escalation] My Zoom application keeps freezing and crashing when I open it, how do I fi", "user_query": "My Zoom application keeps freezing and crashing when I open it, how do I fix it?", "project": "KAN", "status": "OPEN", "priority": "High", "created_at": "2026-09-23 11:36:16 UTC", "reporter": "maya.sharma@meridian.com", "transcript": "* Employee (maya.sharma@meridian.com): My Zoom application keeps freezing and crashing when I open it, how do I fix it?\n* Meridian Assistant: {\"ai_answer\": \"Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide provided, I can provide the following step-by-step guidance to resolve the issue with your Zoom application crashing:\\n\\n1. Clear the Zoom Application Cache:\\n   a. Close the Zoom application completely, including any background processes.\\n   b. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\\\Zoom'' and press Enter.\\n   c. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders).\\n   d. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''.\\n   e. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''.\\n   f. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in.\\n\\n   Note: This will sign you out of Zoom, so you will need to log in again after clearing the cache.\\n\\n2. If the issue persists after clearing the cache, proceed with a clean reinstall of the Zoom application:\\n   a. Uninstall the existing Zoom application.\\n   b. Download the latest version of the Zoom client from the official website.\\n   c. Install the new Zoom client, making sure to allow all necessary permissions and configurations.\\n   d. Launch the Zoom application and sign in.\\n\\nIf the Zoom application continues to crash or freeze even after the cache clearing and clean reinstall, you may need to escalate the issue to L2 Support for further investigation. The troubleshooting guide also mentions that issues like this could be caused by GPU hardware acceleration conflicts or background process locks, so L2 Support may need to perform additional diagnostics.\\n\\nPlease let me know if you have any other questions or if the issue persists after following these steps.\", \"matches\": [{\"score\": 0.5454, \"title\": \"Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\", \"text\": \"* Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart\\n\\n## Troubleshooting Steps\\n\\n### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache\\n\\nZoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\\\Local\\\\Zoom and AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\Zoom. Over time or after improper application termination, these files become corrupted, causing instant crashes when Zoom tries to initialize the UI or join meetings. Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch.\\n\\n**Instructions:**\\n1. Close Zoom completely (check the system tray to ensure no background Zoom processes are running).\\n2. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\\\Zoom'' and press Enter.\\n3. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders).\\n4. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''.\\n5. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''.\\n6. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in.\\n\\n> **Note:** This will sign you out of Zoom. You will need to log in again after clearing the cache.\\n\\n### Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom\"}, {\"score\": 0.5246, \"title\": \"Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\", \"text\": \"* When exactly does the issue occur? (e.g., Immediately upon launching Zoom, during sign-in, when clicking ''Join Meeting'', or when starting video/screen sharing?)\\n* Do you see any error messages or error codes (such as Error 10003, Error 3000, or a Windows application crash notification), or does Zoom close abruptly without warning?\\n* Did this problem start happening recently (for example, following a recent Zoom client update, Windows update, or system reboot)?\\n\\n## Reported Symptoms\\n\\n* Zoom application freezes or becomes unresponsive upon opening\\n* Zoom crashes unexpectedly during or before meetings\\n* Zoom crashes immediately when joining a meeting or starting video\\n* Zoom window turns white or black and closes without an error message\\n* Audio not working in Zoom (can''t hear or be heard)\\n* Video/camera not working in Zoom or showing blank display\\n* Unable to join Zoom meetings with connection timeout\\n* Zoom is running slow, dropping frames, or lagging severely\\n* Screen sharing fails or causes the application to terminate\\n* Zoom login issues or authentication session tokens corrupted\\n* Zoom updates failing or corrupt installation state\\n* Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart\\n\\n## Troubleshooting Steps\\n\\n### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache\\n\\nZoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\\\Local\\\\Zoom and AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\Zoom.\"}, {\"score\": 0.5127, \"title\": \"Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\", \"text\": \"# Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\\n\\n**Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\\n**Page ID:** 5341202\\n**Document ID:** KB-ZOOM-001 | **Category:** Video Conferencing | **Application:** Zoom\\n**Last Updated:** 2026-09-21 | **Author:** IT Support Team\\n\\n**Tags:** zoom, video conferencing, freezing, crashing, audio, video, camera, microphone, cache, not working, can''t join, meeting, lag, slow, black screen, screen share, reinstall, uninstall, corrupt, error 10003, crash on launch\\n\\n## Preliminary Assessment\\n\\nBefore proceeding directly to cache clearing or reinstallation, it is essential to ask the user diagnostic questions to identify whether the crash is triggered by corrupted temporary cache files, GPU hardware acceleration conflicts, or background process locks. In the vast majority of cases where Zoom crashes abruptly during launch or joining calls without a system hardware fault, corrupted local AppData cache files (CEF webcache, meeting session data, and dump logs) are the primary culprit.\\n\\n## Diagnostic Questions\\n\\n* When exactly does the issue occur? (e.g., Immediately upon launching Zoom, during sign-in, when clicking ''Join Meeting'', or when starting video/screen sharing?)\"}, {\"score\": 0.4624, \"title\": \"Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\", \"text\": \"> **Note:** This will sign you out of Zoom. You will need to log in again after clearing the cache.\\n\\n### Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom\\n\\nIf clearing the cache does not resolve the issue, a full clean reinstall removes all Zoom components (including potentially corrupted binaries, DLLs, and registry entries) and installs a fresh copy of the latest version from the Microsoft Store or official package repository. This resolves deep binary corruption, version conflicts, and persistent crashes.\\n\\n**Instructions:**\\n1. Close Zoom completely.\\n2. Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps.\\n3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall.\\n4. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\\\Zoom.\\n5. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package.\\n6. Relaunch Zoom and log in.\\n\\n> **Note:** This will completely remove Zoom and all its settings/data. The application will be reinstalled fresh. Custom settings will need to be reconfigured.\\n\\n### Step 3: Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes\\n\\nA hanging ZoomOpener or CptHost process in Task Manager can lock Zoom data files, preventing a new instance from opening properly.\\n\\n**Instructions:**\\n1. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager.\\n2. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes.\\n3.\"}, {\"score\": 0.4605, \"title\": \"Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202\", \"text\": \"Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''.\\n3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''.\\n4. Restart Zoom.\\n\\n### Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions\\n\\nWindows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media.\\n\\n**Instructions:**\\n1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\\n2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom.\\n3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom.\\n4. Relaunch Zoom.\\n\\n## Escalation Guidelines\\n\\nIf preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies.\"}]}", "note": "Live Jira ticket #KAN-5 successfully created in Atlassian project KAN with full conversation transcript."}','2026-09-23 11:36:16.817202');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(99,29,'user','text','I am Agnish Paul','2026-09-23 11:56:28.446204');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(100,29,'bot','text','Hello, Agnish Paul! I''m the Meridian Enterprise AI Copilot, powered by AWS Bedrock in the ap-south-1 region. It''s a pleasure to meet you. I''d be happy to assist you with a variety of tasks, including Zoom troubleshooting, Windows camera and microphone privacy permissions, invoicing and GST tax code guidelines, as well as Jira L2 Support escalation. Please feel free to let me know how I can be of help. I''m here to provide efficient and professional support.','2026-09-23 11:56:28.446204');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(101,31,'user','text','What are the requirements for a GST 28% invoice?','2026-09-24 08:17:08.899754');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(102,31,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Based on the verified Confluence documentation, here are the key requirements for a GST 28% invoice:\n\n1. Determine if the product falls under the GST 28% tax bracket:\n   - The GST 28% tax bracket is used for newly onboarded product categories.\n   - Check the product''s category listing to confirm if it requires the GST 28% tax code.\n\n2. When raising the invoice:\n   - Select the GST 28% tax code for the product.\n   - Add a Remark on the invoice explaining the classification of the product under the GST 28% bracket.\n   - This is required for audit purposes to justify the use of the higher 28% GST rate.\n\n3. Escalation and support:\n   - If you are unsure which tax code to use, you should raise a query with the Finance Configuration team for guidance.\n   - Missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay the invoice approval process.\n\nIn summary, the key requirements for a GST 28% invoice are:\n1. Confirm the product category requires the GST 28% tax code.\n2. Select the GST 28% tax code and add a Remark explaining the classification.\n3. Escalate to the Finance Configuration team if you need assistance with the tax code selection.", "matches": [{"score": 0.4761, "title": "Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564", "text": "# Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564 **Page ID:** 1474564 ## Content Overview Tax codes determine how tax is calculated and reported for each invoice, based on GST (Goods and Services Tax) brackets. Current tax brackets GST 5%: Applies to select essential product categories. GST 12%: Applies to standard mid-tier products. GST 18%: The default rate for most catalog items. GST 28% (New Category): Used for newly onboarded product categories. Invoices using this bracket require Remarks explaining the classification, for audit purposes. If you''re unsure which code to use Check the product''s category listing, or raise a query with the Finance Configuration team."}, {"score": 0.4183, "title": "Uploading client tax documents", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354", "text": "# Uploading client tax documents\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354 **Page ID:** 2916354 ## Content Overview Each client may have specific tax documentation on file, such as a tax exemption certificate or GST registration certificate for their jurisdiction. Steps Select the client before uploading their document - documents are tied to the specific client, not the invoice. Upload the relevant certificate in the Tax Document field on the Raise Invoice screen. If you switch to a different client, you must upload that client''s own document - a previous client''s file does not carry over. Note Missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay invoice approval."}, {"score": 0.399, "title": "How to raise an invoice for a product", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793", "text": "# How to raise an invoice for a product\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793 **Page ID:** 1441793 ## Content Overview This article explains the standard steps to raise an invoice for any product already configured in the system. Steps Go to Invoices > Raise Invoice. Select the product, enter quantity, and choose the correct tax code. Add any remarks if applicable, then click Submit. Confirm the invoice appears under ''My Invoices'' with status ''Submitted''."}, {"score": 0.392, "title": "Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730", "text": "# Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730 **Page ID:** 1343730 ## Content Overview This article describes the general request process for onboarding a new product into the invoicing catalog. Steps Submit a product onboarding request with product name, category, and applicable tax code. The Configuration team reviews and adds the product to the master data table. Allow up to 2 business days for the product to appear across all invoicing screens. Note If a newly added product does not appear where expected after this window, raise a support ticket rather than resubmitting the request."}, {"score": 0.3824, "title": "Troubleshooting invoice stuck in draft status", "url": "s3://meridia-buck/Troubleshooting_invoice_stuck_in_draft_status.md", "text": "# Troubleshooting: invoice stuck in draft status\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474580 **Page ID:** 1474580 ## Content Problem An invoice remains in Draft status and does not move to Submitted. Steps to resolve Check that all mandatory fields are filled in. Refresh the page and try submitting again. If the issue persists after a refresh, contact support with the invoice ID and a screenshot of any error shown."}]}','2026-09-24 08:17:08.899754');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(103,31,'bot','resolved','{"status": "Resolved", "note": "Employee confirmed query was successfully resolved."}','2026-09-24 08:17:12.385965');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(104,32,'user','text','Validate invoice #1042 for compliance','2026-09-24 08:31:16.055130');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(105,32,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Understood. Let me review the verified Confluence documentation to validate invoice #1042 for compliance.\n\n1. Invoice approval and submission workflow:\n   a. Verify that invoice #1042 has been submitted and is not still in the Draft status.\n   b. Check that the invoice has progressed through the approval stages of Submitted, Under Review, and Approved.\n   c. If the invoice is stuck in the Draft status, follow the troubleshooting steps:\n      i. Ensure all mandatory fields are filled in correctly.\n      ii. Refresh the page and try submitting the invoice again.\n      iii. If the issue persists, contact support with the invoice ID and a screenshot of any error shown.\n\n2. Uploading client tax documents:\n   a. Verify that the correct client''s tax documentation (e.g., tax exemption certificate, GST registration certificate) has been uploaded and associated with invoice #1042.\n   b. Ensure that the tax document is up-to-date and matches the client''s current tax status.\n   c. Note that missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay the approval of invoice #1042.\n\n3. Additional considerations:\n   a. Check for any GST 28% remarks or other specific tax-related requirements that may apply to this invoice.\n   b. Ensure that the invoice complies with any internal SOPs or guidelines regarding invoice processing and approval.\n   c. If escalation to L2 Support is required, follow the appropriate procedures outlined in the documentation.\n\nPlease let me know if you need any clarification or have additional questions regarding the validation of invoice #1042.", "matches": [{"score": 0.4342, "title": "Invoice approval and submission workflow", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441820", "text": "# Invoice approval and submission workflow\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441820 **Page ID:** 1441820 ## Content Overview Once submitted, invoices go through the following stages: Draft, Submitted, Under Review, Approved. Steps Draft invoices can be edited freely. Once submitted, an invoice is locked and routed for review. If rejected, the invoice returns to Draft with reviewer comments."}, {"score": 0.4043, "title": "Uploading client tax documents", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354", "text": "# Uploading client tax documents\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354 **Page ID:** 2916354 ## Content Overview Each client may have specific tax documentation on file, such as a tax exemption certificate or GST registration certificate for their jurisdiction. Steps Select the client before uploading their document - documents are tied to the specific client, not the invoice. Upload the relevant certificate in the Tax Document field on the Raise Invoice screen. If you switch to a different client, you must upload that client''s own document - a previous client''s file does not carry over. Note Missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay invoice approval."}, {"score": 0.3994, "title": "Troubleshooting invoice stuck in draft status", "url": "s3://meridia-buck/Troubleshooting_invoice_stuck_in_draft_status.md", "text": "# Troubleshooting: invoice stuck in draft status\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474580 **Page ID:** 1474580 ## Content Problem An invoice remains in Draft status and does not move to Submitted. Steps to resolve Check that all mandatory fields are filled in. Refresh the page and try submitting again. If the issue persists after a refresh, contact support with the invoice ID and a screenshot of any error shown."}, {"score": 0.3892, "title": "Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564", "text": "# Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564 **Page ID:** 1474564 ## Content Overview Tax codes determine how tax is calculated and reported for each invoice, based on GST (Goods and Services Tax) brackets. Current tax brackets GST 5%: Applies to select essential product categories. GST 12%: Applies to standard mid-tier products. GST 18%: The default rate for most catalog items. GST 28% (New Category): Used for newly onboarded product categories. Invoices using this bracket require Remarks explaining the classification, for audit purposes. If you''re unsure which code to use Check the product''s category listing, or raise a query with the Finance Configuration team."}, {"score": 0.3873, "title": "Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730", "text": "# Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730 **Page ID:** 1343730 ## Content Overview This article describes the general request process for onboarding a new product into the invoicing catalog. Steps Submit a product onboarding request with product name, category, and applicable tax code. The Configuration team reviews and adds the product to the master data table. Allow up to 2 business days for the product to appear across all invoicing screens. Note If a newly added product does not appear where expected after this window, raise a support ticket rather than resubmitting the request."}]}','2026-09-24 08:31:16.055130');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(106,33,'user','text','What are the requirements for a GST 28% invoice?','2026-09-24 09:05:18.874457');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(107,33,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Based on the verified Confluence documentation, here are the key requirements for a GST 28% invoice:\n\n1. Tax Code Selection:\n   - The GST 28% tax code should be selected for invoices involving newly onboarded product categories that fall under the 28% GST bracket.\n   - This higher tax rate applies to certain specialized or luxury product categories.\n\n2. Remarks Requirement:\n   - Invoices using the GST 28% tax code must include remarks explaining the product classification.\n   - The remarks are required for audit purposes, to justify the application of the 28% GST rate.\n\n3. Finance Configuration Team Escalation:\n   - If you are unsure which tax code to use for a particular product, you should raise a query with the Finance Configuration team.\n   - The Finance Configuration team can provide guidance on the correct tax code to apply based on the product category.\n\nIn summary, the key requirements for a GST 28% invoice are:\n1. Use the GST 28% tax code for eligible product categories.\n2. Include remarks explaining the product classification for audit purposes.\n3. Escalate to the Finance Configuration team if you need assistance in determining the correct tax code.", "matches": [{"score": 0.4761, "title": "Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564", "text": "# Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564 **Page ID:** 1474564 ## Content Overview Tax codes determine how tax is calculated and reported for each invoice, based on GST (Goods and Services Tax) brackets. Current tax brackets GST 5%: Applies to select essential product categories. GST 12%: Applies to standard mid-tier products. GST 18%: The default rate for most catalog items. GST 28% (New Category): Used for newly onboarded product categories. Invoices using this bracket require Remarks explaining the classification, for audit purposes. If you''re unsure which code to use Check the product''s category listing, or raise a query with the Finance Configuration team."}, {"score": 0.4183, "title": "Uploading client tax documents", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354", "text": "# Uploading client tax documents\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354 **Page ID:** 2916354 ## Content Overview Each client may have specific tax documentation on file, such as a tax exemption certificate or GST registration certificate for their jurisdiction. Steps Select the client before uploading their document - documents are tied to the specific client, not the invoice. Upload the relevant certificate in the Tax Document field on the Raise Invoice screen. If you switch to a different client, you must upload that client''s own document - a previous client''s file does not carry over. Note Missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay invoice approval."}, {"score": 0.399, "title": "How to raise an invoice for a product", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793", "text": "# How to raise an invoice for a product\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793 **Page ID:** 1441793 ## Content Overview This article explains the standard steps to raise an invoice for any product already configured in the system. Steps Go to Invoices > Raise Invoice. Select the product, enter quantity, and choose the correct tax code. Add any remarks if applicable, then click Submit. Confirm the invoice appears under ''My Invoices'' with status ''Submitted''."}, {"score": 0.392, "title": "Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730", "text": "# Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730 **Page ID:** 1343730 ## Content Overview This article describes the general request process for onboarding a new product into the invoicing catalog. Steps Submit a product onboarding request with product name, category, and applicable tax code. The Configuration team reviews and adds the product to the master data table. Allow up to 2 business days for the product to appear across all invoicing screens. Note If a newly added product does not appear where expected after this window, raise a support ticket rather than resubmitting the request."}, {"score": 0.3824, "title": "Troubleshooting invoice stuck in draft status", "url": "s3://meridia-buck/Troubleshooting_invoice_stuck_in_draft_status.md", "text": "# Troubleshooting: invoice stuck in draft status\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474580 **Page ID:** 1474580 ## Content Problem An invoice remains in Draft status and does not move to Submitted. Steps to resolve Check that all mandatory fields are filled in. Refresh the page and try submitting again. If the issue persists after a refresh, contact support with the invoice ID and a screenshot of any error shown."}]}','2026-09-24 09:05:18.874457');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(108,33,'bot','jira_ticket','{"is_live_jira": true, "ticket_key": "KAN-2317", "ticket_url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/browse/KAN-2317", "summary": "[Support Escalation] What are the requirements for a GST 28% invoice?", "user_query": "What are the requirements for a GST 28% invoice?", "project": "KAN", "status": "TO DO", "priority": "High", "created_at": "2026-09-24 09:05:56 UTC", "reporter": "maya.sharma@meridian.com", "transcript": "* Employee (maya.sharma@meridian.com): What are the requirements for a GST 28% invoice?\n* Meridian Assistant: {\"ai_answer\": \"Based on the verified Confluence documentation, here are the key requirements for a GST 28% invoice:\\n\\n1. Tax Code Selection:\\n   - The GST 28% tax code should be selected for invoices involving newly onboarded product categories that fall under the 28% GST bracket.\\n   - This higher tax rate applies to certain specialized or luxury product categories.\\n\\n2. Remarks Requirement:\\n   - Invoices using the GST 28% tax code must include remarks explaining the product classification.\\n   - The remarks are required for audit purposes, to justify the application of the 28% GST rate.\\n\\n3. Finance Configuration Team Escalation:\\n   - If you are unsure which tax code to use for a particular product, you should raise a query with the Finance Configuration team.\\n   - The Finance Configuration team can provide guidance on the correct tax code to apply based on the product category.\\n\\nIn summary, the key requirements for a GST 28% invoice are:\\n1. Use the GST 28% tax code for eligible product categories.\\n2. Include remarks explaining the product classification for audit purposes.\\n3. Escalate to the Finance Configuration team if you need assistance in determining the correct tax code.\", \"matches\": [{\"score\": 0.4761, \"title\": \"Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564\", \"text\": \"# Understanding tax codes in the invoicing system\\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474564 **Page ID:** 1474564 ## Content Overview Tax codes determine how tax is calculated and reported for each invoice, based on GST (Goods and Services Tax) brackets. Current tax brackets GST 5%: Applies to select essential product categories. GST 12%: Applies to standard mid-tier products. GST 18%: The default rate for most catalog items. GST 28% (New Category): Used for newly onboarded product categories. Invoices using this bracket require Remarks explaining the classification, for audit purposes. If you''re unsure which code to use Check the product''s category listing, or raise a query with the Finance Configuration team.\"}, {\"score\": 0.4183, \"title\": \"Uploading client tax documents\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354\", \"text\": \"# Uploading client tax documents\\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/2916354 **Page ID:** 2916354 ## Content Overview Each client may have specific tax documentation on file, such as a tax exemption certificate or GST registration certificate for their jurisdiction. Steps Select the client before uploading their document - documents are tied to the specific client, not the invoice. Upload the relevant certificate in the Tax Document field on the Raise Invoice screen. If you switch to a different client, you must upload that client''s own document - a previous client''s file does not carry over. Note Missing or mismatched tax documentation may delay invoice approval.\"}, {\"score\": 0.399, \"title\": \"How to raise an invoice for a product\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793\", \"text\": \"# How to raise an invoice for a product\\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1441793 **Page ID:** 1441793 ## Content Overview This article explains the standard steps to raise an invoice for any product already configured in the system. Steps Go to Invoices > Raise Invoice. Select the product, enter quantity, and choose the correct tax code. Add any remarks if applicable, then click Submit. Confirm the invoice appears under ''My Invoices'' with status ''Submitted''.\"}, {\"score\": 0.392, \"title\": \"Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog\", \"url\": \"https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730\", \"text\": \"# Adding a new product to the invoicing catalog\\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1343730 **Page ID:** 1343730 ## Content Overview This article describes the general request process for onboarding a new product into the invoicing catalog. Steps Submit a product onboarding request with product name, category, and applicable tax code. The Configuration team reviews and adds the product to the master data table. Allow up to 2 business days for the product to appear across all invoicing screens. Note If a newly added product does not appear where expected after this window, raise a support ticket rather than resubmitting the request.\"}, {\"score\": 0.3824, \"title\": \"Troubleshooting invoice stuck in draft status\", \"url\": \"s3://meridia-buck/Troubleshooting_invoice_stuck_in_draft_status.md\", \"text\": \"# Troubleshooting: invoice stuck in draft status\\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/1474580 **Page ID:** 1474580 ## Content Problem An invoice remains in Draft status and does not move to Submitted. Steps to resolve Check that all mandatory fields are filled in. Refresh the page and try submitting again. If the issue persists after a refresh, contact support with the invoice ID and a screenshot of any error shown.\"}]}", "note": "Jira ticket #KAN-2317 automatically created and assigned to the L2 Support Desk with query context and chat history."}','2026-09-24 09:05:56.298399');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(109,35,'user','text','My Zoom keeps crashing when I open it. Can you help me fix it?','2026-09-24 17:33:12.038201');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(110,35,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide provided, here are the steps to troubleshoot your Zoom crashing issue:\n\n1. Check for error messages or error codes when Zoom crashes. Note if the issue started recently after a Zoom update, Windows update, or system reboot.\n\n2. Clear the Zoom application cache:\n   a. Locate the Zoom cache folders in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom.\n   b. Delete the contents of these folders to purge any corrupted temporary files.\n   c. Relaunch Zoom and see if the crashing issue is resolved.\n\n3. Test your internet connection:\n   a. Open a web browser and check if you can browse the internet normally.\n   b. Run a speed test to ensure you have at least 3-5 Mbps for video calls.\n   c. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly.\n   d. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or using an Ethernet cable.\n\n4. Disable hardware acceleration in Zoom video settings:\n   a. Open Zoom settings and go to the Video tab.\n   b. Uncheck \"Enable hardware acceleration for video processing\" and \"Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video\".\n   c. Restart Zoom and see if the crashing issue is resolved.\n\n5. Verify audio and camera privacy permissions in Windows:\n   a. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n   b. Under App Permissions, ensure \"Let desktop apps access your camera\" and \"Let desktop apps access your microphone\" are enabled for Zoom.\n   c. Relaunch Zoom.\n\nIf the issue persists after trying these troubleshooting steps, you should escalate the case to L2 Support for further investigation. The support engineer may need to review Windows Event Viewer logs, inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies.\n\n\u26a1 **Automated Remote Remediation Available:**\nI can remotely clear your Zoom application cache on your managed device (**AVDGLS5-4**) via MeshCentral to resolve corrupted cache and crash loops without manual file deletion.\n\nWould you like me to clear your Zoom cache remotely? Simply reply **''Yes, clear the cache''** or click the **Clear Zoom Cache Remotely** button below.", "matches": [{"score": 0.6448, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "* Do you see any error messages or error codes (such as Error 10003, Error 3000, or a Windows application crash notification), or does Zoom close abruptly without warning? * Did this problem start happening recently (for example, following a recent Zoom client update, Windows update, or system reboot)? ## Reported Symptoms * Zoom application freezes or becomes unresponsive upon opening * Zoom crashes unexpectedly during or before meetings * Zoom crashes immediately when joining a meeting or starting video * Zoom window turns white or black and closes without an error message * Audio not working in Zoom (can''t hear or be heard) * Video/camera not working in Zoom or showing blank display * Unable to join Zoom meetings with connection timeout * Zoom is running slow, dropping frames, or lagging severely * Screen sharing fails or causes the application to terminate * Zoom login issues or authentication session tokens corrupted * Zoom updates failing or corrupt installation state * Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart ## Troubleshooting Steps ### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache Zoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom. Over time or after improper application termination, these files become corrupted, causing instant crashes when Zoom tries to initialize the UI or join meetings. Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch."}, {"score": 0.6418, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser. 2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls). 3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly. 4. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or ethernet cable. ### Step 5: Disable Hardware Acceleration in Zoom Video Settings Incompatible or outdated GPU display drivers frequently cause Zoom to crash or display a black/white screen when opening video. **Instructions:** 1. Open Zoom Settings (gear icon in the top right). 2. Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''. 3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''. 4. Restart Zoom. ### Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions Windows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media. **Instructions:** 1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security. 2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom. 3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom. 4."}, {"score": 0.6149, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom. 3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom. 4. Relaunch Zoom. ## Escalation Guidelines If preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies."}, {"score": 0.6143, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps. 3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall. 4. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\Zoom. 5. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package. 6. Relaunch Zoom and log in. > **Note:** This will completely remove Zoom and all its settings/data. The application will be reinstalled fresh. Custom settings will need to be reconfigured. ### Step 3: Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes A hanging ZoomOpener or CptHost process in Task Manager can lock Zoom data files, preventing a new instance from opening properly. **Instructions:** 1. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager. 2. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes. 3. Select each and click ''End Task''. 4. Reopen Zoom from the Start Menu. ### Step 4: Check Network Stability & Firewall Zoom requires an active and stable network connection with open UDP ports. Packet loss or aggressive proxy filters can cause crashes during the meeting handshake. **Instructions:** 1. Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser. 2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls). 3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly. 4."}, {"score": 0.5886, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch. **Instructions:** 1. Close Zoom completely (check the system tray to ensure no background Zoom processes are running). 2. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter. 3. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders). 4. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''. 5. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''. 6. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in. > **Note:** This will sign you out of Zoom. You will need to log in again after clearing the cache. ### Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom If clearing the cache does not resolve the issue, a full clean reinstall removes all Zoom components (including potentially corrupted binaries, DLLs, and registry entries) and installs a fresh copy of the latest version from the Microsoft Store or official package repository. This resolves deep binary corruption, version conflicts, and persistent crashes. **Instructions:** 1. Close Zoom completely. 2. Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps. 3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall. 4."}], "remote_action_available": true}','2026-09-24 17:33:12.038208');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(111,36,'user','text','My Zoom is crashing','2026-09-24 17:33:37.464940');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(112,36,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide provided, here are the steps to troubleshoot your Zoom crashing issue:\n\n1. Identify the Reported Symptoms:\n   - Determine if you see any error messages or error codes when Zoom crashes.\n   - Identify when the problem started occurring (e.g., after a recent Zoom update, Windows update, or system reboot).\n   - Describe the specific behavior you are observing, such as Zoom freezing, crashing unexpectedly, or closing without an error message.\n\n2. Clear the Zoom Application Cache:\n   1. Open the Zoom application settings.\n   2. Navigate to the \"Video\" section and uncheck the options for \"Enable hardware acceleration for video processing\" and \"Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video\".\n   3. Restart the Zoom application.\n\n3. Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions:\n   1. Open the Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n   2. Under \"App Permissions\", select \"Camera\" and ensure \"Let desktop apps access your camera\" is enabled for Zoom.\n   3. Select \"Microphone\" and ensure \"Let desktop apps access your microphone\" is enabled for Zoom.\n\n4. Test Your Internet Connection:\n   1. Open a web browser and navigate to a website to test your internet connection.\n   2. Run a speed test to ensure you have a minimum of 3-5 Mbps for video calls.\n   3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly.\n   4. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or using an Ethernet cable.\n\nIf the issue persists after following these steps, you may need to escalate the issue to L2 Support for further investigation. The documentation mentions that in some cases, corrupted local AppData cache files or GPU hardware acceleration conflicts can cause Zoom to crash abruptly, and the steps provided should help resolve the majority of such issues.\n\n\u26a1 **Automated Remote Remediation Available:**\nI can remotely clear your Zoom application cache on your managed device (**AVDGLS5-4**) via MeshCentral to resolve corrupted cache and crash loops without manual file deletion.\n\nWould you like me to clear your Zoom cache remotely? Simply reply **''Yes, clear the cache''** or click the **Clear Zoom Cache Remotely** button below.", "matches": [{"score": 0.7605, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "* Do you see any error messages or error codes (such as Error 10003, Error 3000, or a Windows application crash notification), or does Zoom close abruptly without warning? * Did this problem start happening recently (for example, following a recent Zoom client update, Windows update, or system reboot)? ## Reported Symptoms * Zoom application freezes or becomes unresponsive upon opening * Zoom crashes unexpectedly during or before meetings * Zoom crashes immediately when joining a meeting or starting video * Zoom window turns white or black and closes without an error message * Audio not working in Zoom (can''t hear or be heard) * Video/camera not working in Zoom or showing blank display * Unable to join Zoom meetings with connection timeout * Zoom is running slow, dropping frames, or lagging severely * Screen sharing fails or causes the application to terminate * Zoom login issues or authentication session tokens corrupted * Zoom updates failing or corrupt installation state * Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart ## Troubleshooting Steps ### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache Zoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom. Over time or after improper application termination, these files become corrupted, causing instant crashes when Zoom tries to initialize the UI or join meetings. Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch."}, {"score": 0.7255, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser. 2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls). 3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly. 4. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or ethernet cable. ### Step 5: Disable Hardware Acceleration in Zoom Video Settings Incompatible or outdated GPU display drivers frequently cause Zoom to crash or display a black/white screen when opening video. **Instructions:** 1. Open Zoom Settings (gear icon in the top right). 2. Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''. 3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''. 4. Restart Zoom. ### Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions Windows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media. **Instructions:** 1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security. 2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom. 3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom. 4."}, {"score": 0.6597, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "# Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide\r **Confluence URL:** https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202 **Page ID:** 5341202 **Document ID:** KB-ZOOM-001 | **Category:** Video Conferencing | **Application:** Zoom **Last Updated:** 2026-09-21 | **Author:** IT Support Team **Tags:** zoom, video conferencing, freezing, crashing, audio, video, camera, microphone, cache, not working, can''t join, meeting, lag, slow, black screen, screen share, reinstall, uninstall, corrupt, error 10003, crash on launch ## Preliminary Assessment Before proceeding directly to cache clearing or reinstallation, it is essential to ask the user diagnostic questions to identify whether the crash is triggered by corrupted temporary cache files, GPU hardware acceleration conflicts, or background process locks. In the vast majority of cases where Zoom crashes abruptly during launch or joining calls without a system hardware fault, corrupted local AppData cache files (CEF webcache, meeting session data, and dump logs) are the primary culprit. ## Diagnostic Questions * When exactly does the issue occur? (e.g., Immediately upon launching Zoom, during sign-in, when clicking ''Join Meeting'', or when starting video/screen sharing?)"}, {"score": 0.6527, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps. 3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall. 4. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\Zoom. 5. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package. 6. Relaunch Zoom and log in. > **Note:** This will completely remove Zoom and all its settings/data. The application will be reinstalled fresh. Custom settings will need to be reconfigured. ### Step 3: Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes A hanging ZoomOpener or CptHost process in Task Manager can lock Zoom data files, preventing a new instance from opening properly. **Instructions:** 1. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager. 2. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes. 3. Select each and click ''End Task''. 4. Reopen Zoom from the Start Menu. ### Step 4: Check Network Stability & Firewall Zoom requires an active and stable network connection with open UDP ports. Packet loss or aggressive proxy filters can cause crashes during the meeting handshake. **Instructions:** 1. Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser. 2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls). 3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly. 4."}, {"score": 0.6423, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom. 3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom. 4. Relaunch Zoom. ## Escalation Guidelines If preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies."}], "remote_action_available": true}','2026-09-24 17:33:37.464946');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(113,37,'user','text','My Zoom keeps crashing when I open it. Can you help me fix it?','2026-09-24 17:34:10.733171');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(114,37,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Certainly, I can help you troubleshoot the Zoom crashing issue. Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide provided, please follow these steps:\n\n1. Check for any error messages or error codes when Zoom crashes. Note if the issue started recently after a Zoom client update, Windows update, or system reboot.\n\n2. Clear the Zoom application cache:\n   a. Locate the Zoom cache folders in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom.\n   b. Delete the contents of these folders to purge any corrupted temporary files.\n   c. Relaunch Zoom and see if the crashing issue is resolved.\n\n3. Test your internet connection:\n   a. Open a web browser and navigate to a website to check your internet connectivity.\n   b. Run a speed test to ensure you have a minimum of 3-5 Mbps for video calls.\n   c. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly.\n   d. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or using an Ethernet cable.\n\n4. Disable hardware acceleration in Zoom video settings:\n   a. Open Zoom Settings and go to the Video tab.\n   b. Uncheck the options for \"Enable hardware acceleration for video processing\" and \"Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video\".\n   c. Restart Zoom and see if the crashing issue is resolved.\n\n5. Verify audio and camera privacy permissions in Windows:\n   a. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n   b. Under App Permissions, ensure \"Let desktop apps access your camera\" and \"Let desktop apps access your microphone\" are enabled for Zoom.\n   c. Relaunch Zoom and check if the issue is resolved.\n\nIf the above troubleshooting steps do not resolve the Zoom crashing issue, you should escalate the problem to a human Support Engineer for further investigation. The Support Engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs, inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies that may be causing the problem.\n\n\u26a1 **Automated Remote Remediation Available:**\nI can remotely clear your Zoom application cache on your managed device (**AVDGLS5-4**) via MeshCentral to resolve corrupted cache and crash loops without manual file deletion.\n\nWould you like me to clear your Zoom cache remotely? Simply reply **''Yes, clear the cache''** or click the **Clear Zoom Cache Remotely** button below.", "matches": [{"score": 0.6448, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "* Do you see any error messages or error codes (such as Error 10003, Error 3000, or a Windows application crash notification), or does Zoom close abruptly without warning? * Did this problem start happening recently (for example, following a recent Zoom client update, Windows update, or system reboot)? ## Reported Symptoms * Zoom application freezes or becomes unresponsive upon opening * Zoom crashes unexpectedly during or before meetings * Zoom crashes immediately when joining a meeting or starting video * Zoom window turns white or black and closes without an error message * Audio not working in Zoom (can''t hear or be heard) * Video/camera not working in Zoom or showing blank display * Unable to join Zoom meetings with connection timeout * Zoom is running slow, dropping frames, or lagging severely * Screen sharing fails or causes the application to terminate * Zoom login issues or authentication session tokens corrupted * Zoom updates failing or corrupt installation state * Zoom keeps crashing even after simple restart ## Troubleshooting Steps ### Step 1: Clear Zoom Application Cache Zoom stores session caches, CEF webcache, thumbnails, avatar caches, and local meeting states in AppData\\Local\\Zoom and AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom. Over time or after improper application termination, these files become corrupted, causing instant crashes when Zoom tries to initialize the UI or join meetings. Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch."}, {"score": 0.6418, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser. 2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls). 3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly. 4. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or ethernet cable. ### Step 5: Disable Hardware Acceleration in Zoom Video Settings Incompatible or outdated GPU display drivers frequently cause Zoom to crash or display a black/white screen when opening video. **Instructions:** 1. Open Zoom Settings (gear icon in the top right). 2. Click ''Video'' in the left sidebar, then click ''Advanced''. 3. Uncheck ''Enable hardware acceleration for video processing'' and ''Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video''. 4. Restart Zoom. ### Step 6: Verify Audio and Camera Privacy Permissions Windows Privacy Settings can block Zoom from accessing microphones and cameras, which can lead to application crashes when starting meeting media. **Instructions:** 1. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security. 2. Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom. 3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom. 4."}, {"score": 0.6149, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Under App Permissions, select ''Camera'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your camera'' is enabled for Zoom. 3. Select ''Microphone'' and ensure ''Let desktop apps access your microphone'' is enabled for Zoom. 4. Relaunch Zoom. ## Escalation Guidelines If preliminary troubleshooting, cache clearing, and clean reinstallation do not resolve the issue, escalate to a human Support Engineer for visual remote desktop inspection. The engineer can review Windows Event Viewer logs (Application crash events 1000/1001), inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies."}, {"score": 0.6143, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps. 3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall. 4. After uninstall, delete leftover folders: %appdata%\\Zoom and %localappdata%\\Zoom. 5. Download and install the latest Zoom Workplace package. 6. Relaunch Zoom and log in. > **Note:** This will completely remove Zoom and all its settings/data. The application will be reinstalled fresh. Custom settings will need to be reconfigured. ### Step 3: Restart Zoom Application and Background Processes A hanging ZoomOpener or CptHost process in Task Manager can lock Zoom data files, preventing a new instance from opening properly. **Instructions:** 1. Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager. 2. Look for any ''Zoom'', ''Zoom Meetings'', ''ZoomOpener'', or ''CptHost'' processes. 3. Select each and click ''End Task''. 4. Reopen Zoom from the Start Menu. ### Step 4: Check Network Stability & Firewall Zoom requires an active and stable network connection with open UDP ports. Packet loss or aggressive proxy filters can cause crashes during the meeting handshake. **Instructions:** 1. Test your internet connection by navigating to a web browser. 2. Run a speed test (minimum 3-5 Mbps recommended for video calls). 3. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly. 4."}, {"score": 0.5886, "title": "Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide", "url": "https://agnishpaul2002.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SELFHELP/pages/5341202", "text": "Clearing the cache purges these corrupted temporary files while preserving the core executable, forcing Zoom to rebuild fresh configuration databases on next launch. **Instructions:** 1. Close Zoom completely (check the system tray to ensure no background Zoom processes are running). 2. Press Win+R, type ''%appdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter. 3. Delete the ''data'', ''logs'', ''report'', and ''dump'' folders (do not touch ''bin'' or ''uninstall'' folders). 4. Press Win+R, type ''%localappdata%\\Zoom'' and press Enter, then delete ''webcache'' and ''temp''. 5. Also check ''%temp%'' and remove any temporary files prefixed with ''Zoom''. 6. Relaunch Zoom and sign back in. > **Note:** This will sign you out of Zoom. You will need to log in again after clearing the cache. ### Step 2: Clean Reinstall Zoom If clearing the cache does not resolve the issue, a full clean reinstall removes all Zoom components (including potentially corrupted binaries, DLLs, and registry entries) and installs a fresh copy of the latest version from the Microsoft Store or official package repository. This resolves deep binary corruption, version conflicts, and persistent crashes. **Instructions:** 1. Close Zoom completely. 2. Open Settings > Apps > Installed Apps. 3. Find ''Zoom'' or ''Zoom Workplace'', click Uninstall. 4."}], "remote_action_available": true}','2026-09-24 17:34:10.733178');
INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(115,37,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "\u26a1 **Zoom Cache Cleared Successfully via MeshCentral**\n\nRemediation script executed on managed device (**AVDGLS5-4**):\n\u2022 Stopped active Zoom background processes (`Zoom`, `ZoomOpener`, `CptHost`)\n\u2022 Purged corrupted application caches and CEF webcache files in `AppData\\Local\\Zoom` and `AppData\\Roaming\\Zoom`\n\u2022 Cleaned temporary lock files\n\n> \ud83d\udca1 **Next Steps:** Please launch Zoom again and sign in.\n\nPlease test if your meetings and application are working normally. **Did this resolve your issue?**", "matches": [], "remote_action_executed": true}','2026-09-24 17:34:16.132444');
