INSERT INTO "chat_messages" VALUES(121,38,'bot','matches','{"ai_answer": "Based on the Zoom Application Troubleshooting Guide provided, here are the steps to troubleshoot your Zoom crashing issue:\n\n1. Check for any error messages or error codes when Zoom crashes. Note if the issue started recently after a Zoom update, Windows update, or system reboot.\n\n2. Clear the Zoom application cache:\n   a. Navigate to the Zoom AppData folders: %AppData%\\Zoom and %LocalAppData%\\Zoom\n   b. Delete all files and folders in these locations to clear the cached data.\n   c. Relaunch Zoom and see if the crashing issue is resolved.\n\n3. Test your internet connection:\n   a. Open a web browser and check if you can browse the internet normally.\n   b. Run a speed test to ensure you have a minimum of 3-5 Mbps for video calls.\n   c. If connected to a VPN or proxy, temporarily disconnect and test Zoom directly.\n   d. If on Wi-Fi, try connecting to a 5GHz band or using an Ethernet cable.\n\n4. Disable hardware acceleration in Zoom video settings:\n   a. Open Zoom Settings and go to the Video tab.\n   b. Uncheck \"Enable hardware acceleration for video processing\" and \"Enable hardware acceleration for receiving video\".\n   c. Restart Zoom and see if the crashing issue is resolved.\n\n5. Verify audio and camera privacy permissions in Windows:\n   a. Open Windows Settings > Privacy & Security.\n   b. Under App Permissions, ensure \"Let desktop apps access your camera\" and \"Let desktop apps access your microphone\" are enabled for Zoom.\n   c. Relaunch Zoom.\n\nIf the issue persists after trying these troubleshooting steps, you should escalate the case to L2 Support for further investigation. The support engineer may need to review Windows Event Viewer logs, inspect driver compatibility, or debug system-level group policies.\n\n\u26a1 **Automated Remote Remediation Available:**\nI can remotely clear your Zoom application cache on your managed device (**AVDGLS5-4**) via MeshCentral